  "articleBody": "It can be incredibly frustrating when you know your business is out there, but customers can't seem to find you on Google Maps. There are several reasons why your listing might not be showing up as prominently as you'd like, and they often come down to how well your business information aligns with Google's core ranking factors: relevance, distance, and prominence.\n\nFirst, let's talk about accuracy and completeness. Just like your Business Profile, your Maps listing needs precise, up-to-date details. If your name, address, phone number, hours, or categories are inconsistent or missing, Google might struggle to understand what you do and where you are. Google won't display your business if it can't confidently recommend you as a relevant option.\n\nThen there's the issue of duplicates. If you've got multiple listings for the same business at the same location, Google gets confused and might suppress all of them. This is a common mistake when businesses treat Maps as a completely separate system. Also, your physical location matters. If you’re using a P.O. Box or a virtual office as your business address, Google might reject your listing because it doesn't represent a legitimate physical presence where customers can find you.\n\nWhat Can Change the Right Choice\n\nYour business type significantly impacts how you should approach Maps optimization. If you have a storefront where customers visit, ensuring your map pin is perfectly accurate is crucial. However, if you're a service-area business (like a plumber or electrician), you shouldn't display a physical address on your profile unless it's genuinely a customer-facing location. Instead, define your service areas clearly. Google wants to provide the most relevant result, and misrepresenting your business type can hinder that. Choosing categories for keywords rather than accuracy is another pitfall; stick to what you genuinely are. Neglecting to monitor reviews and user edits can also cause your listing to become outdated or even incorrect, reducing its prominence.\n\nUltimately, appearing on Google Maps isn't about paying for a spot; it's about providing Google with all the clear, accurate, and consistent information it needs to confidently show your business to local searchers. If your business isn't showing up, a thorough review of these factors is usually the first step to getting things back on track.",
  "datePublished": "2026-09-30"
